<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ali ibn Hammud al-Nasir</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1018">1018</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ali ibn Hammud al-Nasir was a commander of Idrisid descent who seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> in 1016 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fitna-of-al-andalus">Fitna of al-Andalus</a> and proclaimed himself <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, the first non-Umayyad to hold the title in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>. Founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammudid-dynasty">Hammudid dynasty</a>, he ruled until 1018, when he was murdered in his bath by his own servants. His family contested the caliphate with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> for years afterwards.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ali ibn Hammud claimed descent from Idris ibn Abdallah, the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idrisid-dynasty">Idrisid dynasty</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> had fallen into civil war after the largely figurehead caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hisham-ii">Hisham II</a> was usurped by his cousin Muhammad II. Power was then contested between Hisham II, Muhammad II and Sulayman, another great-grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-iii">Abd al-Rahman III</a>. By 1013 Sulayman had captured Córdoba with his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> army after a siege of two and a half years, and Hisham II disappeared, with many believing he had been murdered. Sulayman's Berber troops then carried out revenge killings and destruction against his enemies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>To placate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a> and assert his claim, Sulayman gave territories in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> and north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> to his Berber generals: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceuta">Ceuta</a> went to Ali ibn Hammud, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a>, Arcila and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeciras">Algeciras</a> to his brother al-Qasim ibn Hammud. Ali first posed as loyal, but in late 1013 he declared himself independent and announced that he would free the legitimate caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hisham-ii">Hisham II</a>. He also promoted a story that Hisham, uncertain of his fate during the siege, had named him heir-presumptive and wished his death avenged. In 1014 he crossed the Straits of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a> and took Málaga as a base.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> rulers of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> offered the brothers little resistance. In the summer of 1016 Ali marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> and on 17 June defeated Sulayman's Berber troops in the city. An exhumed body found in the palace was declared to be <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hisham-ii">Hisham II</a>, and Sulayman was charged with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>'s murder and beheaded. On 3 July 1016 Ali was proclaimed caliph with the title al-Nasir.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Ali first tried to reduce the abuses of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> militia against the citizens of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, but without success, and the Córdobans came to regard his regime as pro-Berber. After about eight months he reversed course and let the Berbers again make arrests and seize property and arms, earning a reputation as a scourge of the Córdobans. Khayran al-Amiri, the ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almeria">Almería</a>, and others who wanted a legitimate Umayyad as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> turned against him, and by early 1018 open rebellion had broken out. As Ali prepared to meet it, three of his household servants killed him in the palace baths on 22 March 1018. His brother al-Qasim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">al-Ma'mun</a> succeeded him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ali ibn Hammud al-Nasir” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Córdoba, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.89,-4.78&amp;year=1008" aria-label="Córdoba on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Ali ibn Hammud al-Nasir"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4678 4321h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4678 4321h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=37.89,-4.78&amp;year=1008">What was here in 1008? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
